export const debate = {"id":"wood-dillahunty-god-secular-humanism-2020","number":"151","assessmentModel":"5.6 Sol","assessmentRubric":"Slugfester Reassessment Rubric v2","title":"David Wood vs Matt Dillahunty: God or Secular Humanism?","label":"Moral obligation and human flourishing","date":"2026-08-26","duration":"2 hr 21 min","youtubeUrl":"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=u2SjOn8arv8","motion":"Which gives a better foundation for ethics: God or secular humanism?","sourceNote":"Based on the complete debate transcript. Summaries are condensed for clarity, and quotations are kept brief and faithful to the source.","scoringNote":"Scores are AI-generated estimates of argumentative performance and are intended as transparent, revisable judgments rather than final verdicts.","summary":"Wood pressed categorical obligation and transcendent worth, while Dillahunty more effectively defended revisable secular ethics and exposed gaps in divine grounding.","quotes":{"pro":{"text":"what moral obligations do I have?","context":"Wood asks how welfare-centered ethics can categorically obligate someone who rejects universal well-being as a governing end."},"con":{"text":"God by definition is good. Therefore, God is good.","context":"Dillahunty argues that defining God as the standard of goodness does not independently demonstrate the proposed moral foundation."}},"sides":{"pro":{"name":"Theistic moral realism","speaker":"David Wood","color":"teal"},"con":{"name":"Secular humanism","speaker":"Matt Dillahunty","color":"coral"}},"score":{"pro":79,"con":84},"sections":[{"sectionId":"moral-reality-and-divine-grounding","title":"Objective Morality and Divine Grounding","timebox":"12:35–127:16","score":{"pro":75,"con":88},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-divine-ground-of-values-and-obligations","time":"12:35","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Theism can ground nonphysical moral properties in God and universal obligations in the authoritative relationship between creator and persons.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The constructive directly addresses the motion by connecting objective goodness, universal obligation, moral responsibility, and authoritative personal direction within one comparatively comprehensive theistic framework. Principal limitation: It identifies God as a possible metaphysical ground without sufficiently explaining the grounding relationship, independently establishing divine goodness, or deriving authority from creation rather than merely asserting it. Live burden: The pro side must clarify how divine nature constitutes goodness, why that nature generates obligations for persons, and why competing non-theistic realist foundations remain comparatively inadequate. Locked score: The strong band recognizes excellent motion relevance, coherent conceptual integration, and a readily recoverable thesis, while preserving substantial reservations about evidential warrant, metaphysical specification, unsupported explanatory confidence, and the uncompleted transition from divine existence to binding authority.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-creator-goodness-authority-gap","time":"38:04","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Creative power alone establishes neither goodness nor moral authority, so theism must independently justify God's character, commands, and jurisdiction.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply distinguishes creating persons from possessing moral goodness or legitimate authority over them, exposing separate inferential bridges that the constructive had compressed together. Principal limitation: Contrasting this gap with secular goals and disagreement-resolution methods does not itself establish the categorical authority of those goals or show that practical methodology supplies moral ontology. Live burden: The con side must preserve its successful demand for independent justification while positively explaining why secular commitments generate obligations rather than only workable procedures and socially enforced expectations. Locked score: The very strong band reflects exceptional diagnostic precision, decisive responsiveness, stable terminology, proportionate force, and direct contact with the theistic route's central weakness, with only a residual comparative burden concerning the independent normativity of secular flourishing.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-transcendent-foundation-of-goodness","time":"124:29","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Objective moral facts and universal obligations require a nonphysical, humanity-transcending foundation identified with God's necessarily good being.","score":73,"critique":"Strongest feature: The constructive confronts the metaphysical question directly, arguing that objective moral facts and universal obligations require a foundation beyond physical description, individual preference, and collective human convention. Principal limitation: It does not exclude non-theistic realism or explain why a personal divine being is necessary, while the central expression foundation of goodness remains insufficiently specified. Live burden: The pro side must compare basic, necessary, relational, and other nonphysical realist alternatives, then establish why authority and normativity specifically require the proposed divine nature. Locked score: The mixed band reflects indispensable relevance, a recoverable transcendent-ground thesis, and appropriate acknowledgment of mystery, offset by materially overstated necessity, limited evidential warrant, underdeveloped metaphysics, and insufficient representational charity toward competing forms of objective moral realism.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-divine-goodness-circularity","time":"126:46","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Calling God the standard of goodness risks circularly assuming the objective divine foundation that the theistic case needed to demonstrate.","score":87,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply precisely identifies the danger that defining God as goodness merely restates the desired conclusion, leaving the proposed objective foundation independently undemonstrated. Principal limitation: A substantive necessary-nature identity need not be formally circular merely because goodness is constitutive of God, so the objection occasionally treats definition and metaphysical identity too similarly. Live burden: The con side must distinguish trivial stipulation from substantive identity while continuing to demand independent motivation for divine goodness and an explanation of its obligation-generating authority. Locked score: The very strong band reflects decisive target contact, exceptional responsiveness, stable terminology, strong evidential support, and an accurately located assumption risk, moderated only by slightly excessive certainty and incomplete accommodation of the strongest divine-nature formulation.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"flourishing-consequences-and-obligation","title":"Flourishing, Consequences, and Binding Obligation","timebox":"18:31–64:25","score":{"pro":87,"con":79},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-goal-conditionality-objection","time":"27:25","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Welfare-based ethics remains conditional if rejecting its objective removes obligation, and harmful goals can make horrific conduct instrumentally effective.","score":85,"critique":"Strongest feature: The objection precisely isolates the difference between effective means toward an accepted goal and categorical obligation, then stress-tests consequential reasoning through a dissenter committed to destructive purposes. Principal limitation: It overstates the secular position by suggesting that rejection leaves morality entirely speechless, overlooking possible arguments from vulnerability, reciprocity, social participation, and reasons applicable independently of personal motivation. Live burden: The pro side must show why those secular considerations remain merely prudential or institutional, while preserving the distinction between lacking motivation and lacking morally authoritative reasons. Locked score: The very strong band reflects decisive contact, unusually clear scope, forceful diagnostic structure, and direct pressure on the central secular bridge, tempered by an insufficiently charitable characterization of available secular resources and slightly excessive inferential force.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-shared-goals-physical-consequences","time":"18:31","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Given shared human aims, physical consequences permit objective comparisons between choices and identify better or worse means for advancing flourishing.","score":76,"critique":"Strongest feature: The chess analogy intelligibly shows how objective physical consequences can distinguish more effective from less effective conduct once participants share a determinate practical objective. Principal limitation: The reasoning moves too quickly from objectively assessable means toward objectively authoritative moral ends, leaving the categorical status of the governing commitment to well-being insufficiently justified. Live burden: The con side must explain why flourishing supplies reasons that bind dissenters, not merely conditional recommendations, while separately answering the evolutionary challenge concerning the reliability of naturalistic moral cognition. Locked score: The strong band reflects coherent instrumental reasoning, direct contact with the secular-foundation burden, and commendable real-time qualification, balanced against partial responsiveness, limited normative warrant, unstable foundational scope, and incomplete engagement with the distinct epistemological objection.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-obligation-for-welfare-dissenter","time":"62:23","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Effective guidance toward flourishing does not establish a genuine moral obligation for someone who rejects concern for human well-being.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply states with exceptional clarity that instrumental effectiveness after accepting an end differs from a genuine obligation applicable to someone who rejects that end altogether. Principal limitation: Framing the secular position through the wholly resistant dissenter risks underexamining objective reasons grounded in vulnerability, reciprocity, relationship, and participation that need not depend upon subjective endorsement. Live burden: The pro side must demonstrate why those purported reasons lack moral authority rather than merely observing that an agent can remain motivationally unmoved by them. Locked score: The very strong band reflects precise formulation, proportionate questioning, complete responsiveness, excellent burden relevance, and an important concession regarding effective conditional guidance, with remaining uncertainty centered on whether the objection defeats every sophisticated secular account or primarily exposes an incompletely articulated bridge.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-social-participation-reciprocal-obligation","time":"57:36","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Unavoidable social participation, objectively different consequences, and benefits received from others support reciprocal obligations even when individuals reject flourishing.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly addresses the dissenter through unavoidable interdependence, objective comparisons among social outcomes, and reciprocity arising from substantial benefits received within cooperative institutions. Principal limitation: The transition from participation and benefit to a binding moral should remains compressed, because reciprocity functions as a normative principle whose own authority cannot simply be presupposed. Live burden: The con side must justify fair reciprocity independently, explain its application to involuntary participation and unequally distributed benefits, and distinguish genuine obligation from enforceable social expectation. Locked score: The strong band reflects complete contact with the dissenter case, practical plausibility, significant central relevance, and a coherent multi-part response, moderated by an underdeveloped normativity bridge and somewhat stronger claims of obligation than the supplied considerations independently warrant.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"human-value-rights-and-cooperation","title":"Human Value, Rights, and Cooperation","timebox":"25:30–140:23","score":{"pro":75,"con":87},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-image-of-god-rights-and-worth","time":"25:30","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Creation and sustenance by God make morality reality-responsive, while bearing God's image provides persons with rights and morally significant worth.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply supplies a unified reality-based account of obligation and human status, directly contrasting merely conditional goal pursuit with rights grounded in the divine image. Principal limitation: The asserted movement from image-bearing to determinate rights remains compressed, because neither the relevant characteristics nor the mechanism producing equal entitlements receives sufficient specification. Live burden: The pro side must identify which rights follow from divine image, explain why every person possesses them equally, and establish their content without depending on disputed interpretive assumptions. Locked score: The strong band recognizes direct burden contact, substantial responsiveness, stable overall scope, and an intelligible theological alternative, while registering limited evidential support, partly unstable terminology, implicit qualifications, and an underdeveloped inference from created resemblance or status to concrete universal protections.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-mortal-life-relational-significance","time":"76:24","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Mortality leaves powerful reasons to protect life because future agency, relationships, contributions, and effects on others remain deeply significant.","score":89,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply directly explains why mortality does not erase value, grounding serious reasons against premature death in future agency, relationships, contributions, and enduring effects upon others. Principal limitation: Relational significance establishes substantial concern but does not automatically secure equal intrinsic worth or universal rights for isolated, disliked, incapacitated, or minimally contributing persons. Live burden: The con side must show how its relational account protects every person comparably when social attachment, anticipated contribution, preference satisfaction, and reciprocal usefulness differ dramatically. Locked score: The very strong band recognizes a clear, proportionate, fully responsive, and intuitively powerful answer to the mortality challenge, while reserving the unresolved foundational question of whether variable relationships can support equal protection without importing a more intrinsic conception of status.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-human-justice-fits-image-of-god","time":"88:26","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Human special value and responsibility for justice fit image-of-God anthropology better than an atheistic description of humanity alone.","score":71,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply identifies a potentially illuminating explanatory fit between human practices of valuing persons, imposing justice, and an anthropology that assigns humanity distinctive image-bearing status. Principal limitation: It treats atheism too broadly as having nothing relevant to say about human nature or value, overlooking relational, naturalistic, constructivist, and non-theistic realist explanations already available. Live burden: The pro side must compare specific rival accounts and show why image-bearing explains justice better, rather than inferring theological status from the existence of human evaluative practices. Locked score: The mixed band reflects genuine relevance, recoverable comparative intent, and meaningful contact with human worth, but also substantially overstated contrast, indeterminate terminology, inadequate evidential support, and insufficient charity toward secular accounts that ground responsibility without claiming metaphysical greatness.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-relational-value-empathy-cooperation","time":"136:54","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Relational value, shared interests, empathy, and cooperation can justify concern for persons without requiring universe-wide intrinsic human value.","score":86,"critique":"Strongest feature: The constructive carefully rejects value existing independently of every valuer while preserving substantial human significance through self-concern, relationships, empathy, shared interests, effects, and cooperation. Principal limitation: Relational value may vary with attachment, ability, popularity, or contribution, creating difficulty explaining equal protection for isolated, despised, dependent, or minimally reciprocal persons. Live burden: The con side must derive stable universal safeguards from its relational framework and explain why concern for everyone follows when particular relationships and cooperative benefits are radically unequal. Locked score: The very strong band reflects a clear, proportionate, coherent, and centrally relevant secular account that distinguishes relational from intrinsic value without denying significance, tempered by the unresolved question of equal status where actual valuation and reciprocal participation become exceptionally weak.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"moral-knowledge-disagreement-and-correction","title":"Moral Knowledge, Disagreement, and Correction","timebox":"9:59–129:12","score":{"pro":76,"con":84},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-evolutionary-debunking-of-moral-cognition","time":"9:59","role":"Load-bearing constructive","words":"Evolutionary and social explanations may account for moral convictions without showing that those convictions accurately represent objective moral reality.","score":74,"critique":"Strongest feature: The argument clearly distinguishes a causal genealogy of moral conviction from truth, directly engaging the indispensable epistemological question of whether naturalistic cognition reliably corresponds to independent moral facts. Principal limitation: Evolutionary influence alone does not establish unreliability, because selection, reflection, interpersonal correction, and evidence-sensitive deliberation could produce substantially dependable faculties despite their survival-related origins. Live burden: The pro side must demonstrate that naturalistic formation is systematically insensitive to moral truth, while addressing the strongest account on which evolved capacities become increasingly reliable through criticism and cooperative inquiry. Locked score: The mixed band reflects exceptional relevance and a recoverable challenge, moderated by materially overstated force, incomplete qualification, compressed warranting, and insufficient engagement with the principal reliability alternative.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-evidence-corrects-harmful-means","time":"50:46","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Evidence and internal consistency can reveal when ostensibly beneficial conduct actually conflicts with a professed commitment to human well-being.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply candidly accepts human fallibility while explaining how empirical consequences and internal consistency can expose genocidal means that contradict a professed commitment to well-being. Principal limitation: Evidence most directly corrects factual mistakes and inconsistencies, but cannot by itself resolve every disagreement between coherent yet morally antagonistic conceptions of value or flourishing. Live burden: The con side must specify the normative standards governing evidence, explain their authority, and address agents whose harmful commitments remain internally consistent after all relevant consequences are understood. Locked score: The strong band recognizes a clear, proportionate, and substantially complete answer to the error-correction challenge, supported by methodological humility and direct responsiveness, while retaining reservations about limited empirical jurisdiction and an assumed resolution of disputes that may concern values rather than facts.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-divinely-truth-directed-intuitions","time":"98:26","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Theism can portray fallible moral intuitions as divinely produced perceptions directed toward truth rather than only evolutionarily useful dispositions.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply carefully presents moral intuition as fallible yet potentially reality-directed, offering a coherent theistic model that distinguishes truth-tracking from reproductive or social usefulness. Principal limitation: The model assumes the relevant good creator and does not independently show that divine formation is more probable than naturalistic reliability developed through experience, reflection, and correction. Live burden: The pro side must justify its creator premise, explain persistent moral disagreement, and compare predictive or explanatory advantages against the strongest naturalistic account of dependable cognition. Locked score: The strong band recognizes explicit qualification, stable conceptual distinctions, appropriate modal force, and substantial engagement with the epistemic problem, while reflecting partial responsiveness and the unresolved dependence of the proposed truth-directed genealogy on earlier contested claims about divine existence and goodness.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-fallible-revisable-moral-inquiry","time":"127:29","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Secular moral inquiry can acknowledge error and use evidence, discussion, and continuing revision rather than majority opinion or proclamations of certainty.","score":83,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply articulates an admirably fallibilist method in which evidence, consistency, criticism, and continuing revision improve moral judgment without reducing conclusions to majority preference or certainty. Principal limitation: A successful epistemic procedure does not itself provide an ontological foundation for objective goodness or demonstrate that divine truth-direction is explanatorily inferior. Live burden: The con side must explain which standards guide revision, why those standards are authoritative, and how methodological improvement connects to the broader comparative claim about ethical foundations. Locked score: The strong band recognizes conceptual clarity, explicit qualification, proportionate force, substantial contact with naturalistic reliability concerns, and a persuasive account of correction, while reflecting partial responsiveness because the distinct metaphysical grounding demand remains uncontacted and theistic fallibilism remains compatible with the method.","tags":[]}}]},{"sectionId":"motivation-atrocity-and-practical-application","title":"Motivation, Atrocity, and Practical Application","timebox":"35:48–136:34","score":{"pro":79,"con":81},"exchanges":[{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-theistic-moral-transformation","time":"79:48","role":"Supporting reply","words":"Theistic conversion can transform conduct by replacing human insignificance with recognition of objective duties toward persons who bear God's image.","score":82,"critique":"Strongest feature: The autobiographical example gives concrete support to the carefully limited proposition that adopting theism can transform conduct by reinterpreting other persons as bearers of objective significance. Principal limitation: Practical transformation supplies evidence about motivational efficacy, not the truth of divine-image metaphysics, and one experience cannot establish general motivational superiority over secular commitments. Live burden: The pro side must connect transformation to the comparative foundation without treating beneficial consequences as independent confirmation of the theological beliefs producing them. Locked score: The strong band reflects a recoverable, appropriately qualified possibility claim, meaningful contact with motivational symmetry, and evidence adequate for the personal example, balanced against limited generalizability and the continuing separation between changed behavior and the ontological accuracy of the worldview credited for that change.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-unavoidable-life-motivation-symmetry","time":"35:48","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Social life has unavoidable consequences, while invoking God neither guarantees motivation in indifferent agents nor prevents religiously rationalized atrocities.","score":77,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply sharply distinguishes society's judgment of obligation from the motivational reach of moral argument, then argues that invoking God encounters the same wholly indifferent agent, providing contact with challenged claims and a symmetry grounded in the supplied exchange. Principal limitation: Treating complete indifference as a shared motivational endpoint does not resolve whether secular and theistic accounts differ in the existence, authority, or categorical character of obligations, and the absolute impossibility language exceeds what this excerpt demonstrates. Live burden: Establish why motivational symmetry answers the authority question, while preserving the important distinction between ignored obligations and nonexistent obligations. Locked score: This remains a strong, responsive reply whose reach is narrower than its universal phrasing suggests.","tags":[]}},{"pro":{"ledgerMoveId":"pro-covenant-limits-on-biblical-violence","time":"116:12","role":"Supporting constructive","words":"Ancient covenant penalties do not authorize present Christians to kill, although God may possess just authority to end human lives.","score":75,"critique":"Strongest feature: The move expressly distinguishes an ancient, land-specific covenant from present Christian authorization, thereby supplying a clear practical limitation against contemporary imitation of biblical penalties. Principal limitation: Restricting present authorization does not vindicate the ancient commands, and the stronger assertion that God justly ends lives receives considerably less warrant than its argumentative force requires. Live burden: The pro side must explain why divine authorship makes the original violence just, identify principled constraints on interpretation, and connect those answers to the comparative ethical foundation. Locked score: The strong band recognizes recoverable scope, useful historical qualification, stable practical application, and direct engagement with religious violence, while preserving significant reservations about the unestablished justice premise and the difference between denying contemporary permission and defending the underlying commands.","tags":[]},"con":{"ledgerMoveId":"con-due-process-proportionate-intervention","time":"134:55","role":"Major direct reply","words":"Secular humanism can constrain responses to grave threats through autonomy, due process, collective risk assessment, and proportionate intervention.","score":84,"critique":"Strongest feature: The reply presents a coherent institutional progression from individual autonomy through due process and collective risk assessment to proportionate intervention against genuinely grave threats. Principal limitation: This practical governance framework does not directly determine whether ancient covenantal violence was just, and its institutional example supplies limited evidence about implementation under contested conditions. Live burden: The con side must specify safeguards, thresholds, and accountability mechanisms while explaining how the proposed procedures remain governed by humanistic values when public judgments become fearful or majoritarian. Locked score: The strong band reflects excellent coherence, clear scope, proportionate possibility language, and complete contact with the practical-governance component, balanced against subsidiary rather than central burden relevance and incomplete engagement with the historical justice question posed by the opposing covenant claim.","tags":[]}}]}],"overall":{"pro":{"score":79,"strengths":["Landed a precise and forceful distinction between conditional guidance toward accepted goals and obligations that purport to bind a welfare dissenter.","Connected objective moral facts, authority, human worth, and moral cognition within a unified theistic route directly relevant to the motion.","Qualified moral intuition as fallible and distinguished ancient covenantal authorization from permissions claimed by present Christians.","Used the dissenter and harmful-end cases to expose a genuine normative bridge that the secular account needed to articulate more fully."],"blunders":[{"text":"The proposed divine ground was repeatedly identified more clearly than it was demonstrated, leaving goodness, authority, and the exclusion of non-theistic realism underdeveloped.","links":[]},{"text":"The contrast with atheism sometimes became too sweeping, particularly when secular accounts of human nature, value, and cognitive reliability received insufficient consideration.","links":[]}]},"con":{"score":84,"strengths":["Landed the debate's strongest diagnostic reply by separating creative power from independently established goodness, commands, and moral authority.","Presented a fallible, evidence-sensitive, and revisable method for moral inquiry without reducing ethical judgment to majority opinion.","Explained mortal human significance through relationships, future agency, contributions, and effects with clear and proportionate claims.","Supplied practical secular resources including reciprocity, cooperation, due process, collective risk assessment, and proportionate intervention."],"blunders":[{"text":"The shared-goal analogy established objective means-end comparisons more successfully than it established the categorical authority of well-being itself.","links":[]},{"text":"Reciprocity and unavoidable social participation were invoked as obligation-generating considerations without fully justifying the binding normative principle connecting them.","links":[]}]}},"logicalExtension":{"pro":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that theism offers a comparatively unified explanation of objective moral truth, categorical authority, equal personal worth, and defeasible moral knowledge when these are grounded in a necessarily good personal reality rather than contingent human endorsement.","premises":["If moral obligations are categorical, their authority cannot depend entirely on whether each affected person happens to endorse welfare, reciprocity, cooperation, or any other contingent goal.","A necessarily good personal ground could unite moral standards with legitimate authority if goodness is substantively constitutive of that nature and creation establishes responsibility without reducing goodness to power.","Equal human worth follows more securely if every person bears the same fundamental relation to a good creator, irrespective of popularity, ability, contribution, reciprocity, or present social valuation.","Theistic moral cognition need only be defeasibly reliable: divinely grounded capacities may remain fallible while evidence, reflection, experience, and interpersonal criticism correct particular judgments."],"conclusion":"Therefore, if these substantive claims about necessary goodness, legitimate creator-responsibility, equal status, and defeasible reliability are independently supported, theism would provide a more unified foundation for categorical ethics than endorsement-dependent secular alternatives."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Invariant status under relational loss","text":"A foundation should protect persons precisely when ordinary sources of relational value collapse. An individual may become isolated, hated, unconscious, severely disabled, or unable to reciprocate, yet moral protection should not diminish with social attachment or expected contribution. A shared creator-relation offers an invariant status condition: relationships can enrich the reasons for care without creating the person's basic standing. This does not independently prove theism, but it supplies a comparative test that a secular relational account must meet with equally stable protections."},{"title":"Authority and accountability convergence","text":"Ethical foundations are stronger when standards, authority, and accountability converge without becoming identical. On the steelmanned view, necessary goodness determines the standard, personal creation establishes a morally significant relationship, and accountability answers the practical question of why obligations concern particular agents. Each element still requires defense, but separating them avoids deriving goodness from power or authority from definition. The resulting structure also clarifies what evidence would count against the account: incoherence among divine character, purported commands, and equal personal worth."}]},"con":{"finalArgument":{"thesis":"The AI steelman is that secular humanism can ground action-guiding ethics in impartial reasons arising from sentient vulnerability, agency, reciprocity, and shared social conditions while using public, fallible methods to correct judgments without positing divine authority.","premises":["Sentient beings have standpoint-independent reasons to avoid severe suffering, coercion, and destroyed agency because those conditions frustrate interests that exist whether or not an aggressor endorses them.","Impartiality requires comparable interests to receive comparable consideration, preventing personal rejection of flourishing from erasing the claims of everyone exposed to that person's choices.","Reciprocity strengthens but does not create basic standing: social benefits generate additional duties, while vulnerability and agency protect persons unable to contribute or participate reciprocally.","Public evidence, consistency testing, testimony, due process, and revision provide accountable methods for applying these standards, even though no method eliminates moral error or disagreement."],"conclusion":"Therefore, secular humanism can provide non-arbitrary moral reasons and publicly revisable guidance if it defends impartial consideration of vulnerability and agency, rather than treating well-being as merely an optional collective preference."},"newArguments":[{"title":"Reasons indexed to affected interests","text":"A dissenter's refusal to value welfare need not erase moral reasons, because reasons can be indexed to everyone affected rather than only to the acting person's preferences. Severe injury frustrates another person's agency, projects, relationships, and capacity for experience; those losses remain real facts about that person's standpoint. Impartial deliberation asks whether comparable interests are treated comparably across persons. The dissenter may remain unmoved, but motivational resistance no more cancels those reasons than disbelief about physical consequences cancels the consequences themselves."},{"title":"Public corrigibility as comparative strength","text":"A moral framework gains practical authority when its applications can be challenged through reasons accessible to differently situated people. Evidence about harm, testimony from affected communities, consistency across similar cases, transparent procedures, and reversible policies create multiple opportunities for correction. This does not transform methodology into metaphysical proof, but it establishes a comparative advantage over disputed commands whose interpretation depends on inaccessible revelation or contested authority. Secular humanism can therefore claim institutional reliability modestly, while leaving deeper metaethical questions open to continued argument."}]}},"year":2020};
